      "writtenOn": "2020-03-26T21:58:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Why didn\u0027t this work? \n\nThis behavior (corrupting only the cached hash) is better because the original hash can be restored (by resetting Cr50 or reloading the hash from NVMEM).\n\nHash cache needs to be updated only when NVMEM is written (by AP) and Cr50 should refer to always the cached hash when it talks to EC or AP. \n\nWas there some bug in the cache handling code? I think we should fix that.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "33639598dc4485034eb16505d069005ea76989ca",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "26cdaeb6_a3a89752",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 7
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1283719
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-03-26T22:21:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "There are three copies of EC-RW hash in Cr50.\nH_1 in a EC-EFS module, H_2 in a kernel secdata in nvmem cache(volatile), and H_3 in a kernel secdata in nvmem (non-volatile).\n\nWhen AP accesses kernel secdata through TPM vendor command, it is H_2 (and H_3) through TPM task.\nWhen EP requests for verification through EC-COMM, it is H_1.\nWhen H_2 (and H_3) are updated, then TPM tasks makes EFS module to reload H_1 from H_2.\n\nIn a NO_BOOT test case, it was not enough to corrupt H_1 only, because AP reads H_2, finds no changes, and does nothing. I had to corrupt H_2.\n\nThe reason why this patch corrupts H_3 is because AP would update H_2 anyway in recovery, and it will be overwritten H_3 eventually. Meanwhile, CR50 doesn\u0027t have any API to release a lock on dirty nvmem cache without a commit.",
      "parentUuid": "fd994a2b_c043fe49",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 18,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 66
      },
      "revId": "33639598dc4485034eb16505d069005ea76989ca",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
